          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is G47 a code, a category or a block, and how would I tell the difference?",
          "answer": "**G47 is a category, not a full code and not a block.**\n\n- **Block:** The chapter groups codes into ranges. G40 to G47 is a block of episodic and paroxysmal disorders in the nervous system chapter, covering epilepsy, migraine, headaches and sleep disorders. (Source: *ICD-10-CM Chapter 6: diseases of the nervous system*)\n- **Category:** G47 is one three-character heading inside that block. It holds sleep disorders such as insomnia, hypersomnia, sleep apnea, narcolepsy and parasomnias. (Source: *Coding insomnia: G47 and F51*)\n- **Code:** A full code is the complete, most specific entry. The code-structure guidance says most categories are not valid at three characters and must be extended with more characters, so G47 alone is usually a heading rather than a final code. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*)\n\nTo tell them apart, count the characters and check whether more follow. A range like G40–G47 is a block. A three-character entry like G47 is a category. A longer entry with the extra characters filled in is a code. The coding guidance also says to look a code up in the Alphabetic Index and then confirm it in the Tabular List, because the Tabular List carries the notes that govern use.\n\nThe passages did not list G47's extended subcodes, so I can't tell you which full code would apply to any particular situation. A medical coder or the official ICD-10-CM tabular list would give you that.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
